    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** serving as our clear offensive anchor. Facing Feltner (xERA 4.96), **Corbin Carroll** earns an elite 85.94 score, benefiting from a high-upside matchup against a vulnerable arm and a +8% bump for hitting in the #2 hole. Flanking him in the outfield, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) is a priority start against Kelly, Me. The combination of a favorable SP matchup and a significant +14% platoon edge makes **Mickey Moniak** one of our most efficient sources of production today. Meanwhile, **Gabriel Moreno** (71.17) and **Brice Turang** (67.91) provide stability; **Brice Turang** in particular remains vital given the league-wide scarcity of stolen bases, as his ability to leverage speed against Ray (L) keeps him firmly in our starting rotation despite the minor platoon disadvantage.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical concerns regarding our middle-infield depth. **Max Muncy** (43.71) barely survives the cut today; his score is dragged down significantly by the -19% SP skill penalty against Rasmussen, making him our most marginal start. Had **Jose Caballero** been confirmed in his team\u2019s starting lineup, he likely would have displaced **Max Muncy** to capitalize on a higher volatility ceiling. We are also closely monitoring the injury report, as the absence of **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** continues to strain our bench depth. While **Isaac Paredes** (67.11) offers a solid floor against Houser, we need to see more production from the bottom of our order\u2014specifically **Austin Riley** (58.02), who is currently hindered by an unfavorable #8 spot in the lineup\u2014to keep pace in the Roto standings. We remain ready to cycle in prospects like **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, or **Henry Bolte** should our current starters fail to deliver against these projected mid-tier matchups.",
